/**
 * \page BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay Signal display
__________________________________________________________________

Detailed description
__________________________________________________________________

 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Description|
This plugin displays incoming signals as a single graph in which every channel is drawn as its own
horizontally stacked trace. The horizontal axis is time; each channel gets a lane, and the 
vertical position within a lane is the signal amplitude.

To display your signal correctly and as smoothly as possible, you may need to configure this box. Please see below for examples.

 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Description|
__________________________________________________________________

Inputs description
__________________________________________________________________

 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Inputs|
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Inputs|

 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Input1|
Data to display. Signal and Streamed Matrix types are supported.
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Input1|

 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Input2|
Stimulations.
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Input2|
 
 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Input3|
Measurement units of the data.
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Input3|
__________________________________________________________________

Settings description
__________________________________________________________________

 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Settings|
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Settings|

 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting1|
Type of display, can be either Scan mode or Scroll mode. In Scan mode, the new data arrives
from left to right and the displayed data does not move; a sweep cursor marks the write position.
In Scroll mode, the new data always arrives on the right and all displayed data moves right to left.
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting1|

 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting2|
Automatic vertical (y) scaling mode: Per channel, By type, Global, or None. See the examples below.
An automatic scale is worked out again for every new page: in Scan mode when the cursor starts again
at the left, in Scroll mode every Time Scale. It then fits the page just drawn. While the first page
fills, it is worked out twice a second.
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting2|
 
 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting3|
Amplitude that fills half a channel lane, in signal units, when automatic vertical scaling mode is
None. With a value of 100, a trace touching the top of
its lane is at +100 units.
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting3|
 
 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting4|
DC level subtracted from every sample before it is scaled, i.e. the signal value drawn on the lane
baseline, when automatic vertical scaling mode is None. Channels are separated by the stacking
itself, so this is a baseline correction and not a channel spacing control.
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting4|
 
 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting5|
Size of time window to display, in seconds.
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting5|
 
 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting6|
Enable bottom (x) ruler?
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting6|

 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting7|
Enable the left amplitude ruler: a per-lane bracket showing the amplitude the lane spans.
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting7|

 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting8|
Draw every channel on a single shared baseline instead of one lane each. This is the 'butterfly
plot'. It is the same renderer as the stacked view, with all baselines collapsed to mid-height.
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Setting8|

__________________________________________________________________

Online visualization settings
__________________________________________________________________

 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_OnlineVisualizationSettings|
A parameter panel is shown to the left of the graph during playback.

  - Display Mode : switches between Scan and Scroll.
  - Auto vertical scale : Per channel, By type, Global, or None.
  - Time Scale : the time span displayed. Changing it resizes the buffer and clears history.
  - Vertical Scale : the amplitude filling half a channel lane. Greyed out unless auto scaling is None.
  - Vertical Offset : DC level removed before scaling. Greyed out unless auto scaling is None.
  - Bottom ruler / Left ruler : toggle the time ruler and the amplitude scale bar.
  - Multiview : overlay every channel on a single baseline.

Three further entries exist only in this panel and are not box settings, so they are not saved with
the scenario:

  - Channels : show or hide individual channels. Select all, Deselect all and Invert selection change
    them all at once.
  - Channels per page : 0 fits every channel in the view; a smaller value keeps each lane that tall
    and makes the view scroll, which is what high channel counts need. Greyed out in Multiview.
  - Colour per channel : colour each trace rather than drawing them all alike.
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_OnlineVisualizationSettings|
__________________________________________________________________

Examples description
__________________________________________________________________

 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Examples|

To get some signals displayed right, changing the automatic y scaling may be useful. The provided scaling settings are 

	- Per channel (default) : Every channel will get centered and scaled separately. This should allow viewing arbitrary signals. This is suitable for unfiltered data which may have huge differences in scale and DC between channels.
	- By type : The EEG channels share one scale, the EOG channels another and the digital (trigger, counter) channels a third, so that amplitudes can be compared within each kind. Other channels, such as ECG, EMG, GSR, or unnamed BIP and AUX inputs, keep a scale of their own. The kind is read from the channel name: names with EOG (HEOG, VEOG) are EOG, Trigger, SampleCounter, Digi, DIG and Saw are digital, and electrode names are EEG.
	- Global : All channels are put to the same scale which is the maximum of all the channels. This mode is suitable for filtered EEG and data which may have long-term trends but where all channels generally stay in the same range. 
	- None: User must specify global center and scale. Display is never automatically rescaled. This is suitable for filtered EEG data where the meaningful range is within known parameters and DC doesn't vary.

In every automatic mode each channel is centred on its own signal, so a DC offset does not push it out of its lane; By type and Global share only the scale. The scale is rounded up to 1, 2 or 5 times a power of ten, so the left ruler reads as ±50 or ±200.

Practical example :

 OpenViBE has a tutorial scenario bundled : box-tutorials/signal-display.xml

 An easy way to test this box consists of generating signals with the Sinus Oscillator plugin (found under 'Data generation' in Designer), and then connecting its signal output to the signal input of the Signal Display plugin.

 \image html signaldisplay_online.png "Visualizing 2 channels using the Signal Display plugin."
 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Examples|

__________________________________________________________________

Miscellaneous description
__________________________________________________________________

 * |OVP_DocBegin_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Miscellaneous|

Best practices

All vertical scaling modes except 'None' work the scale out again for every new page, from the page
just drawn. The whole displayed window is redrawn at the new scale, so the trace stays internally
consistent.

For most situations the following is recommended:

	- First filter the signals meaningfully to remove DC and slow trends by using the Temporal Filter box. For EEG, you can use a bandpass configuration with a range a such as [1,40] hz.
	- Next, set the vertical scaling to 'None' and provide the display box a meaningful fixed scale corresponding to your signal. You can find out a suitable scale by activating the left ruler while the auto scale is active and reading the amplitude each lane spans. Note that when the scale is given in the box parameters, the scale can be saved with the scenario.

Known issues and workarounds

The box supports only two data stream types: Matrix and Signal. Many OpenViBE 
stream types derive from the Streamed Matrix type. In this case, you can 
configure the box to have a Streamed Matrix input socket, and then connect the 
derived stream type to that. Note that the data of the stream not contained in the 
matrix will be ignored. In any case, you may be able to visualize something 
meaningful. In the case of Feature Vector stream, you can try to use the 
Matrix Transpose box on the feature stream first to turn the features into 
channels.

Multiview presents all the signals in the same space. With automatic scaling each trace is still
centred on its own signal; with 'None' scaling, channels with large DC differences may overlap
unhelpfully, so filter the signal first.

 * |OVP_DocEnd_BoxAlgorithm_SignalDisplay_Miscellaneous|
 */
